age of <MATH_0039> by <MATH_0040>,\n<STRUCT_0008> <MATH_0041> is the restriction of\n<MATH_0042> to <MATH_0043>,\n<STRUCT_0009> <MATH_0044> is the composition of\n<MATH_0045> with <MATH_0046>.\n<STRUCT_0010>\nThis triple satisfies the compatibility condition: first we always\nobtain <MATH_0047> and <MATH_0048> for free\n(see discussion preceding Lemma <REF_0005>).\nThen we just define <MATH_0049> to be the composition\nof <MATH_0050> and the morphism <MATH_0051>.\nWe omit the verification that this works.\n\n<STRUCT_0011><STRUCT_0012>\nIf <MATH_0052> is an <ACCENT_0001>tale morphism where <MATH_0053> is a scheme, then\nwe obtain <MATH_0054>.\nOf course, if <MATH_0055> is a further <ACCENT_0002>tale morphism of schemes,\nthen <MATH_0056> pulled back to <MATH_0057> corresponds to\n<MATH_0058>.\nThe closed subset <MATH_0059> is just defined as the closed\nsubset such that <MATH_0060> for <MATH_0061>\npulls <MATH_0062> back to <MATH_0063>\n\n<STRUCT_0013><STRUCT_0014>\nConsider Noetherian schemes <MATH_0064> over <MATH_0065> and a morphism\n<MATH_0066> corresponding to <MATH_0067> as above.\nThen we see that <MATH_0068> is set theoretically contained in <MATH_0069>\nif and only if <MATH_0070> (as topological spaces). Hence we see that\n<MATH_0071> agrees with <MATH_0072> as a functor. This produces the isomorphism\n<MATH_0073>. (We've omitted a small detail here which is\nthat for the locally Noetherian formal algebraic spaces <MATH_0074> and\n<MATH_0075> it suffices to check one gets an isomorphism after evaluating\non locally Noetherian schemes over <MATH_0076>.)\n\n<STRUCT_0015><STRUCT_0016>\nWe omit the proof of conditions (1), (2), and (3).\n<STRUCT_0017>", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d42eb380d54d8fe217dd9add2c69a6a35c0d1fa6c5d6fbae2cedeb9cd398d94d", "unit_id": "inventory:artin:0GHC:proof/0001"}}}, {"context_id": "sha256:a9c4ce98bf88d9e13470f87eba0f5fabea11c2fcbd76f08f93ff13de278223df", "hash": "sha256:a9c4ce98bf88d9e13470f87eba0f5fabea11c2fcbd76f08f93ff13de278223d